QStringList does the same, but personally I think it is a design mistake. Not big but a bit annoying. 

From C++ point of view QByteArrayList is a different type that QList<QByteArray> which means that it may misses few optimizations that are meant for a QList. That happens because of such code.

 template<class T> foo () { ....}
 template<class T> foo<QList<T> >() {};

With your implementation the generic code would be picked. I believe QByteArrayList, as well as QStringList should be a QList specialization.

