Yes. I know this violates the API design style, but it is required to be so, for two important reasons. First, as noted in the docs, you can't thread-safely copy these two objects efficiently: system() has 4 bytes  of useful data in a 2596-byte object, while global() cannot be thread-safely trivially copied. To make it thread-safely copyable, I'd have to remove the trivial copyability.

Second, you need an lvalue in order to write:

        std::uniform_real_distribution dist(1, 2.5);
        return dist(QRandomGenerator::global());

or

        return std::generate_canonical<qreal, std::numeric_limits<qreal>::digits>(QRandomGenerator::system());

Or any of the other <random> engines or adaptors.

Because of the anti-pattern

  QList<int> a{1, 2, 3};
  auto i = a.begin();
  QList<int> b = a;
  *i = 42;
  // now b[0] == 42

I have no opinions of this kind of changes apart from the fact that either no such hand-an-internal-reference-out-method on a Qt container (*) does this, or all those methods mark as unshareable. Inconsistency is a big NO-NO-NO-NO.

(*) including QString, QByteArray, etc.

